  "live_records": {
    "q-34074": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34074",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $Q(2,4,1)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨-6,2,9⟩=5$, find the shortest distance from $P(6,-1,0)$ to $Π.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,4,1⟩-⟨6,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-6,2,9⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-4)\\cdot (-6)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 9|}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|24+10+9|}{\\sqrt{36+4+81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|43|}{\\sqrt{121}} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
