    "q-80117": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80117",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,4,-4)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $6x-3y+6z=-18.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=2$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3y+6z & =-18 \\\\ 6(1)-3(2)+6z & =-18 \\\\ 6z & =-18 \\\\ z & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,2,-3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,2,-3⟩-⟨5,4,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨6,-3,6⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-4)\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 6|}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-24+6+6|}{\\sqrt{36+9+36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,-12\\,|}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
