    "q-129120": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129120",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that $Q(5,-1,9)$ lies on the plane $Π:r\\cdot ⟨3,-6,-2⟩=3$, find the shortest distance from $P(7,-2,8)$ to $Π.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,9⟩-⟨7,-2,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨3,-6,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-2)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6-6-2|}{\\sqrt{9+36+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-14|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-129120-a",
